# Swift Testing Rules for Cursor

When generating Swift test files, always use the Swift Testing framework.
Never use XCTest patterns. Follow these rules exactly.

## Required imports
- Use `import Testing` — never `import XCTest`
- Use `@testable import ModuleName` as usual

## Test structure
- Use `@Suite struct` — never `class XxxTests: XCTestCase`
- Use `init()` and `deinit` — never `setUp()` or `tearDown()`
- Mark the whole suite `@MainActor` when testing @MainActor-isolated ViewModels

## Test functions
- Mark with `@Test` — never prefix with `test`
- Name descriptively: `func loadsUsersSuccessfully()` not `func testLoadUsers()`
- Use `async throws` for any test that uses await or can throw

## Assertions
- Use `#expect(condition)` — never XCTAssert*
- Use `try #require(optional)` — never XCTUnwrap
- Use `#expect(throws: ErrorType.self) { }` — never XCTAssertThrowsError
- Use `Issue.record("msg")` — never XCTFail

## Async
- Mark async tests `async throws` — never use `expectation(description:)` or `waitForExpectations`
- Use `await confirmation("label") { confirm in ... }` for callback/notification-based tests

## Parameterized tests
- Use `@Test(arguments: [...])` — never repeat test methods for multiple inputs
- Use `@Test(arguments: MyEnum.allCases)` for enum-driven scenarios

## Reference
See SwiftTesting-Agent-Skill/SKILL.md for full patterns.
